WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.782
Film Courage: Can you give us five filmmaking
tips for anyone who wants to make a movie?

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:05.960
Alex Munoz, Filmmaker and Founder of FYI Films:
First of all have a good screenplay. That’s

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.120
the most important thing. Have a good screenplay.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.969
Number 2 - make sure you have the resources
to make the film. Don’t rush into making

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.640
the film unless you have the resources to
pay for the camera equipment, the locations,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.890
and personnel.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.099
The third one is have a really good producer
who knows that they are doing and be very,

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:29.419
very selection in who you hire.

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:35.550
So have a good script, have the resources
to make the film, hire a really, really good

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:43.930
producer, be polite and respectful and grateful
to every crew member and cast member. Very,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:49.239
very, very important. One way you can show
gratitude is by feeding people well, keep

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:59.589
the morale up. And number five be open to
suggestions. I did a film with Rena Owen,

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:08.640
a really great actress from ONCE WERE WARRIORS.
She is on another plane…her acting…it’s

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:18.450
not mental…it’s the character. It’s
amazing. And so I would block things out and

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.830
she would say “I was thinking Oma would
do it this way.” And I said “Yes, you’re

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.060
right. Let’s do it.”

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.350
You have to be open. Don’t let your ego
get in the way of the process because that

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.211
will really mess you up. If you’re working
with an actress who has been acting for 30,

00:01:35.211 --> 00:01:39.150
40 years and you’ve been directing for five
years, listen to that actress. So be open

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.032
to taking guidance or suggestions because
it will make you feel better.

00:01:43.032 --> 00:01:47.450
Film Courage: What was the biggest mistake
you made in the beginning on a film set?

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:57.060
Alex: So I directed a a music video for a
company called Oil Factory and I got assigned

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:04.070
a cinematographer. I did not get to pick him.
I would ask him to do something and he would

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:10.330
not do it. I would say “Film it this way.”
And he would film it another way. And the

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.030
mistake I made was I didn’t stand up for
my vision. I said “Well I guess this is

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:20.219
what I have to do?” I was just out of film
school and I guess he’s a boor and sometimes

00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:23.890
you have to work with boors.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:28.950
The music video did not end up being a good
video. Why? Because he had no interest in

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:35.420
executing my vision. I don’t know what this
guy was about but the mistake I made was not

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:42.900
standing up for my vision in front of everybody.
I was too shy about it. I’ll never make

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.099
that mistake again.

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:52.120
The same thing…Well I wont talk about that.
But you have to defend your vision. You must

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.450
defend your vision. If there is someone that
is not helping you execute the vision, cut

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:05.689
them off. Because the video was a nightmare,
but I learned from it. That will never happen

00:03:05.689 --> 00:03:05.989
again.

